b. Ben and Dex both scored 92, the highest score in the class, but under the default rank() they did NOT both simply get rank “1” or rank “7” as a whole number. Explain exactly what value they received by default and why R computes it that way for tied values.

  They each received a default rank value of **6.5** because R's `rank()` function sorts values in ascending order (from lowest to highest)—placing the two highest scores of 92 in the 6th and 7th positions—and resolves ties using `ties.method = "average"`, which computes the arithmetic mean of their positions (`(6 + 7) / 2 = 6.5`).


